{:edb/db-before-t 1595 :edb/db-after-t 1596 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534908 50 #inst "2026-10-12T22:41:28.746Z" 13194139534908 17592186045417 true] [17592186192390 1071 "Suppose $X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_{10}$ is an I.I.D random sample where $X_i\\sim \\text{Bernoulli}(\\theta)$ with unknown probability of success $\\theta$. For a particular sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_{10}$, you're given that there are $7$ successes. Find the log-likelihood function of the sample." 13194139534908 17592186045417 false] [17592186192390 1071 "Suppose $X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_{10}$ is an I.I.D random sample where $X_i\\sim \\text{Bernoulli}(\\theta)$ with unknown probability of success $\\theta.$ For a particular sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_{10},$ you're given that there are $7$ successes. Find the log-likelihood function of the sample." 13194139534908 17592186045417 true] [17592186192390 1074 "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x; \\theta)$, where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nl(θ) &= \\ln L(θ) \\\\\n&= \\ln (∏_(i = 1)^(n)f(x_{i} \\\\\n&= θ)) \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)\\ln (f(x_{i} \\\\\n&= θ))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given a sample of $n=10$ independent Bernoulli random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta$. Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta) = \\theta^x(1-\\theta)^{1-x}, \\qquad x=0, 1\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nl(θ) &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)\\ln (θ^{x_{i}}(1 - θ)^{1 - x_{i}}) \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)\\ln (θ^{x_{i}}) + \\ln ((1 - θ)^{1 - x_{i}}) \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}\\ln θ + (1 - x_{i})\\ln (1 - θ) \\\\\n&= \\ln θ∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} + \\ln (1 - θ)∑_(i = 1)^(n)(1 - x_{i}) \\\\\n&= S\\ln θ + (n - S)\\ln (1 - θ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle S = \\sum_ {i=1}^n x_i\n$$\n\nis the number of successes in the sample.\n\nFinally, since there are $7$ successes in the sample, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nl(θ) &= 7\\ln θ + (10 - 7)\\ln (1 - θ) \\\\\n&= 7\\ln θ + 3\\ln (1 - θ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534908 17592186045417 false] [17592186192390 1074 "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x; \\theta),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given a sample of $n=10$ independent Bernoulli random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta.$ Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta) = \\theta^x(1-\\theta)^{1-x}, \\qquad x=0, 1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(1-{x}_{i})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(1-{x}_{i}) \\\\  & =S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\displaystyle S = \\sum_ {i=1}^n x_i$ is the number of successes in the sample.\n\nFinally, since there are $7$ successes in the sample, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =7\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(10-7)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534908 17592186045417 true] [17592186192519 1071 "Suppose $X_1, X_2, X_3$ is an I.I.D random sample with $X_i \\sim B(5,\\theta)$ with unknown probability of success $\\theta$. You're given the particular sample\n$x_1 = 4, \\qquad x_2 = 1, \\qquad x_3 = 1$.\nFind the log-likelihood function of this sample.\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a binomial random variable is given by*\n$f(x) = \\displaystyle \\binom{5}{x} \\theta^x (1-\\theta)^{5-x}, \\qquad x = 0,1, \\ldots, 5$." 13194139534908 17592186045417 false] [17592186192519 1071 "Suppose $X_1, X_2, X_3$ is an I.I.D random sample with $X_i \\sim B(5,\\theta)$ with unknown probability of success $\\theta.$ You're given the particular sample \n\n$$\nx_1 = 4, \\qquad x_2 = 1, \\qquad x_3 = 1.\n$$\n\nFind the log-likelihood function of this sample.\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a binomial random variable is given by*\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\displaystyle \\binom{5}{x} \\theta^x (1-\\theta)^{5-x}, \\qquad x = 0,1, \\ldots, 5.\n$$" 13194139534908 17592186045417 true] [17592186192519 1074 "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x; \\theta)$, where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nl(θ) &= \\ln L(θ) \\\\\n&= \\ln (∏_(i = 1)^(n)f(x_{i}, θ)) \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)\\ln (f(x_{i}, θ))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n = 3$ independent binomial random variables with an unknown probability of success $\\theta$ and $N = 5$ as the number of trials. Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x, θ) &= (\\frac{N}{x})θ^{x}(1 - θ)^{N - x}, x = 0, 1, …, N \\\\\n&= (\\frac{5}{x})θ^{x}(1 - θ)^{5 - x}, x = 0, 1, …, 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nl(θ) &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)\\ln ((\\frac{5}{x_{i}})θ^{x_{i}}(1 - θ)^{5 - x_{i}}) \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(\\ln (\\frac{5}{x_{i}}) + \\ln (θ^{x_{i}}) + \\ln ((1 - θ)^{5 - x_{i}})) \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)\\ln (\\frac{5}{x_{i}}) + ∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}\\ln θ + ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(5 - x_{i})\\ln (1 - θ) \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)\\ln (\\frac{5}{x_{i}}) + \\ln θ∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} + \\ln (1 - θ)∑_(i = 1)^(n)(5 - x_{i}) \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)\\ln (\\frac{5}{x_{i}}) + S\\ln θ + (5n - S)\\ln (1 - θ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle S = \\sum_ {i=1}^n x_i\n$$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(i = 1)^(n)\\ln (\\frac{5}{x_{i}}) &= \\ln (\\frac{5}{x_{1}}) + \\ln (\\frac{5}{x_{2}}) + \\ln (\\frac{5}{x_{3}}) \\\\\n&= \\ln (∏_(i = 1)^(n)(\\frac{5}{x_{i}}))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for $l(\\theta)$ becomes\n\n$$\nl(\\theta) = \\ln\\left(\\prod_{i=1}^n {5 \\choose x_i}\\right) + S\\ln \\theta + (5n - S) \\ln(1-\\theta)\n$$\n\nNow, using the sample data with $n=3$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∏_(i = 1)^(n)(\\frac{5}{x_{i}}) &= (\\frac{5}{4}) \\cdot (\\frac{5}{1}) \\cdot (\\frac{5}{1}) \\\\\n&= 5 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 5 \\\\\n&= 125\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nS = 4 + 1 + 1 = 6\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above values into our expression for $l(\\theta)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nl(θ) &= \\ln (125) + 6\\ln θ + (5 \\cdot 3 - 6)\\ln (1 - θ) \\\\\n&= \\ln 125 + 6\\ln θ + 9\\ln (1 - θ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534908 17592186045417 false] [17592186192519 1074 "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x; \\theta),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i},\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i},\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n = 3$ independent binomial random variables with an unknown probability of success $\\theta$ and $N = 5$ as the number of trials. Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,\\theta ) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{N}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{N-x},\\,x=0,1,…,N \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{5-x},\\,x=0,1,…,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{5-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-\\theta )^{5-{x}_{i}})) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}})+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(5-{x}_{i})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(5-{x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}})+S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(5n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\displaystyle S = \\sum_ {i=1}^n x_i.$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{1}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{2}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{3}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for $l(\\theta)$ becomes\n\n$$\nl(\\theta) =  \\ln\\left( \\prod_{i=1}^n {5 \\choose x_i}\\right) + S\\ln \\theta + (5n - S) \\ln(1-\\theta).\n$$\n\nNow, using the sample data with $n=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}}) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{4})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =125,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nS = 4 + 1 + 1 = 6.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above values into our expression for $l(\\theta)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(125)+6\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(5\\cdot 3-6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡125+6\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534908 17592186045417 true] [17592186192520 1071 "Suppose $X_1,X_2,X_3$ is an I.I.D random sample with $X_i\\sim \\mathrm{Po}(\\theta)$ with unknown rate parameter $\\theta$. You're given the particular sample\n$x_1 = 3,\\qquad x_2=4,\\qquad x_3 = 1$.\n\nFind the log-likelihood function of the sample." 13194139534908 17592186045417 false] [17592186192520 1071 "Suppose $X_1,X_2,X_3$ is an I.I.D random sample with $X_i\\sim \\mathrm{Po}(\\theta)$ with unknown rate parameter $\\theta.$ You're given the particular sample\n\n$$\nx_1 = 3,\\qquad x_2=4,\\qquad x_3 = 1.\n$$\n\nFind the log-likelihood function of the sample." 13194139534908 17592186045417 true] [17592186192520 1074 "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x; \\theta)$, where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nl(θ) &= \\ln L(θ) \\\\\n&= \\ln (∏_(i = 1)^(n)f(x_{i}, θ)) \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)\\ln (f(x_{i}, θ))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=3$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta$. Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x, θ) &= e^{- θ} \\cdot \\frac{θ^{x}}{x!}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nl(θ) &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)\\ln (e^{- θ} \\cdot \\frac{θ^{x_{i}}}{x_{i}!}) \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)\\ln (e^{- θ}) + \\ln (θ^{x_{i}}) - \\ln (x_{i}!) \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(- θ) + ∑_(i = 1)^(n)\\ln (θ^{x_{i}}) - ∑_(i = 1)^(n)\\ln (x_{i}!) \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(- θ) + ∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}\\ln (θ) - ∑_(i = 1)^(n)\\ln (x_{i}!) \\\\\n&= - θ∑_(i = 1)^(n)1 + \\ln θ∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} - ∑_(i = 1)^(n)\\ln (x_{i}!) \\\\\n&=-nθ + \\ln θ∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} - \\ln (∏_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}!)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the sample data with $n=3$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∏_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}! &= 3! \\cdot 4! \\cdot 1! = 6 \\cdot 24 \\cdot 1 = 144, \\\\\n∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} &= 3 + 4 + 1 = 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting the above values into our expression for $l(\\theta)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nl(θ) &= -3θ + 8\\ln θ - \\ln 144\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534908 17592186045417 false] [17592186192520 1074 "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x; \\theta),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i},\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i},\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=3$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta.$ Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,\\theta ) & ={e}^{-\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{\\theta }^{x}}{x!}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}}{{x}_{i}!}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta })+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\theta )+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\theta )+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta )-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & =-\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & =-n\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}!)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the sample data with $n=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}! & =3!\\cdot 4!\\cdot 1!=6\\cdot 24\\cdot 1=144, \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =3+4+1=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting the above values into our expression for $l(\\theta),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =-3\\theta +8\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡144.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534908 17592186045417 true] [17592186192685 1038 #edb/ref 17592186443709 13194139534908 17592186045417 true] [17592186192685 1038 #edb/ref 17592186443711 13194139534908 17592186045417 true] [17592186192685 1038 #edb/ref 17592186443713 13194139534908 17592186045417 true] [17592186192685 1038 #edb/ref 17592186443716 13194139534908 17592186045417 true] [17592186192685 1038 #edb/ref 17592186443720 13194139534908 17592186045417 true] [17592186192795 1038 #edb/ref 17592186443710 13194139534908 17592186045417 true] [17592186192795 1038 #edb/ref 17592186443712 13194139534908 17592186045417 true] [17592186192795 1038 #edb/ref 17592186443718 13194139534908 17592186045417 true] [17592186192795 1038 #edb/ref 17592186443719 13194139534908 17592186045417 true] [17592186192795 1038 #edb/ref 17592186443721 13194139534908 17592186045417 true] [17592186192796 1038 #edb/ref 17592186443714 13194139534908 17592186045417 true] [17592186192796 1038 #edb/ref 17592186443715 13194139534908 17592186045417 true] [17592186192796 1038 #edb/ref 17592186443717 13194139534908 17592186045417 true] [17592186192796 1038 #edb/ref 17592186443722 13194139534908 17592186045417 true] [17592186192796 1038 #edb/ref 17592186443723 13194139534908 17592186045417 true] [17592186192933 1050 720.0 13194139534908 17592186045417 true] [17592186193898 1064 "Recall that if $X_1, \\: X_2, \\: \\ldots, \\: X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x; \\theta)$, where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, \\: x_2, \\: \\ldots, \\: x_n$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = \\prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i;\\,\\theta)\n$$\n\nFor example, if\n\n$X_1,\\quad X_2,\\quad X_3,\\quad X_4,\\quad X_5,\\quad X_6$\nis an I.I.D random sample with $X_i\\sim \\text{Bernoulli}(\\theta)$, where $\\theta$ is the unknown probability of success, then the likelihood function of the sample\n\n$$\nx_1=0, \\quad x_2=1, \\quad x_3=1, \\quad x_4=0, \\quad x_5=1, \\quad x_6=1\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = \\theta^{4}(1-\\theta)^{2}\n$$\n\nThe likelihood function contains lots of products, which can be cumbersome to work with. To make things easier, we convert the products into sums by finding the natural logarithm of $L(\\theta)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nl(θ) &= \\ln (L(θ)) \\\\\n&= \\ln (∏_(i = 1)^(n)f(x_{i} \\\\\n&= θ)) \\\\\n&= \\ln (f(x_{1} \\\\\n&= θ) \\cdot f(x_{2} \\\\\n&= θ)⋯f(x_{n} \\\\\n&= θ)) \\\\\n&= \\ln (f(x_{1} \\\\\n&= θ)) + \\ln (f(x_{2} \\\\\n&= θ)) + ⋯ + \\ln (f(x_{n} \\\\\n&= θ)) \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)\\ln (f(x_{i} \\\\\n&= θ))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis new function $l(\\theta)$ is called the **log-likelihood function** of the sample.\n\nFor our Bernoulli example above, the corresponding log-likelihood function is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nl(θ) &= \\ln (θ^{4}(1 - θ)^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\ln (θ^{4}) + \\ln ((1 - θ)^{2}) \\\\\n&= 4\\ln θ + 2\\ln (1 - θ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534908 17592186045417 false] [17592186193898 1064 "Recall that if $X_1, \\: X_2, \\: \\ldots, \\: X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x; \\theta),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, \\: x_2, \\: \\ldots, \\: x_n$ is \n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = \\prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i;\\,\\theta).\n$$\n\nFor example, if \n\n$$\nX_1,\\quad X_2,\\quad X_3,\\quad X_4,\\quad X_5,\\quad X_6\n$$\n\nis an I.I.D random sample with $X_i\\sim \\text{Bernoulli}(\\theta),$ where $\\theta$ is the unknown probability of success, then the likelihood function of the sample \n\n$$\nx_1=0, \\quad x_2=1, \\quad x_3=1, \\quad x_4=0, \\quad x_5=1, \\quad x_6=1\n$$\n\n \n\nis given by \n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = \\theta^{4}(1-\\theta)^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe likelihood function contains lots of products, which can be cumbersome to work with. To make things easier, we convert the products into sums by finding the natural logarithm of $L(\\theta),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(L(\\theta )) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta )) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{1};\\,\\theta )\\cdot f({x}_{2};\\,\\theta )⋯f({x}_{n};\\,\\theta )) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{1};\\,\\theta ))+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{2};\\,\\theta ))+⋯+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{n};\\,\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis new function $l(\\theta)$ is called the **log-likelihood function** of the sample.\n\nFor our Bernoulli example above, the corresponding log-likelihood function is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{4}(1-\\theta )^{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{4})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-\\theta )^{2}) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534908 17592186045417 true] [17592186193901 1064 "The graph of the likelihood function\n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = \\theta^4(1\\!-\\!\\theta)^2\n$$\n\nfor the Bernoulli distribution we saw earlier is shown below.\n\n![](images/28/28c73749a566d5531ef2f13c5b01bd1d26ced28e19749abf056841492fb497f5.png)\n\nThe graph of the corresponding log-likelihood function\n\n$$\nl(\\theta) = 4 \\ln \\theta + 2 \\ln(1-\\theta)\n$$\n\nis shown below.\n\n![](images/f7/f7f0ba3147504869e162dd371f98d5d6c492e89345f80c75037bdc82a6006d1c.png)\n\nAlthough the graphs look quite different, they both have a maximum at the same point\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\widehat{\\,\\theta\\,}\n$$\n\nThis will become important later.\n\nLet's see more examples of finding the log-likelihood functions for different distributions and samples." 13194139534908 17592186045417 false] [17592186193901 1064 "The graph of the likelihood function \n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = \\theta^4(1\\!-\\!\\theta)^2\n$$\n\nfor the Bernoulli distribution we saw earlier is shown below. \n\n![](images/28/28c73749a566d5531ef2f13c5b01bd1d26ced28e19749abf056841492fb497f5.png)\n\nThe graph of the corresponding log-likelihood function\n\n$$\nl(\\theta) = 4 \\ln \\theta + 2 \\ln(1-\\theta)\n$$\n\nis shown below.\n\n![](images/f7/f7f0ba3147504869e162dd371f98d5d6c492e89345f80c75037bdc82a6006d1c.png)\n\nAlthough the graphs look quite different, they both have a maximum at the same point $\\theta = \\widehat{\\,\\theta\\,}.$ This will become important later. \n\nLet's see more examples of finding the log-likelihood functions for different distributions and samples." 13194139534908 17592186045417 true] [17592186443619 1080 "400" 13194139534908 17592186045417 true] [17592186443619 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534908 17592186045417 true] [17592186443619 1083 #uuid "00ba373d-cc1a-530b-a296-6e2025248701" 13194139534908 17592186045417 true] [17592186443620 1080 "392" 13194139534908 17592186045417 true] [17592186443620 1082 