ned}\n$$\n\nWe are given a sample of $n=15$ independent Bernoulli random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta .$ Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )={\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{1-x},\\,x=0,1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(1-{x}_{i})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(1-{x}_{i}) \\\\  & =S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ is the number of successes in the sample.\n\nFinally, since there are $6$ successes in the sample, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(15-6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534908 17592186045417 true] [17592186443710 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443707 13194139534908 17592186045417 true] [17592186443710 1069 "q-159890" 13194139534908 17592186045417 true] [17592186443710 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534908 17592186045417 true] [17592186443710 1071 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼B(4,\\theta )$ with unknown probability of success $\\theta .$ You're given the particular sample \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=1,\\,{x}_{2}=2,\\,{x}_{3}=1,\\,{x}_{4}=3.\n$$\n\nThe log-likelihood function of the sample is given by\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=\\operatorname{ln}⁡a+b\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +c\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a binomial random variable is given by*\n